@@ -0,0 +1,112 @@
+# RSpec suite optimization notes
+
+## Model specs: verified result
+
+- Baseline command: `bundle exec rspec spec/models --format progress --profile 50`
+- Baseline wall time: 140.894 seconds (Bash `time`)
+- Baseline RSpec time: 138.7 seconds (`Finished in 2 minutes 18.7 seconds`)
+- Baseline examples: 1795, 0 failures, seed 46174
+- Final wall time: 63.895 seconds
+- Final RSpec time: 61.72 seconds (`Finished in 1 minute 1.72 seconds`)
+- Final examples: 1795, 0 failures, seed 34691
+- Wall-clock improvement: 54.6%
+- RSpec-reported improvement: 55.5%
+- Target met: model specs are more than twice as fast overall.
+
+## Setup changes used
+
+- Model specs now use fake Sidekiq mode as the suite default. Explicit `:sidekiq_fake` contexts retain fake mode, and the few mail-delivery examples use `:sidekiq_inline` so assertions that require synchronous delivery remain valid.
+- ActiveRecord SQL logging is disabled in the test RSpec process. SQL query matchers continue to work through notifications; model specs do not assert the SQL log itself.
+- Route reload and initial cache/Redis cleanup run at suite setup instead of for every example.
+- Redis cleanup uses `flushdb` and Rails cache/Redis cleanup is derived only for model files that use Redis or Rails.cache heavily. Other model examples no longer pay that cost.
+- The all-example Resolv DNS mock is skipped for model specs and routes reload once at suite setup.
+- No app runtime code was changed in the setup phase. Factory callback changes are recorded in the follow-up section below.
+
+## Profile findings
+
+- Baseline slow groups included `AccountStatusesCleanupPolicy` (13.26s / 36 examples), `TagFeed` (11.2s / 33 examples), `Status` (10.6s / 62 examples), `PublicFeed` (7.22s / 43 examples), media processing, trend refresh/reblog setup, and notification pagination/preload setup.
+- Final profile still lists feed, status, cleanup-policy, media, and notification work as the largest remaining costs, but those are now individual test setup costs rather than suite-wide per-example hooks.
+- A second shared-fixture refactor for trend/status and cleanup-policy specs was tested and reverted because it leaked shared database records; individual test setup remains unchanged.
+- Direct Redis model specs were concentrated in `async_refresh_spec.rb` and `home_feed_spec.rb`; cache-sensitive model specs were tagged for cache cleanup.
+
+## Run policy
+
+- For the model optimization phase, run only `rspec spec/models`.
+- Do not run the whole suite or multiple spec directories together.
+- Do not run system or streaming specs.
+- Re-run this baseline/profile after future test infrastructure or factory changes.
+
+## Request specs: verified result
+
+- Baseline command: `bundle exec rspec spec/requests --format progress --profile 50`
+- Baseline wall time: 176 seconds
+- Baseline RSpec time: 173.5 seconds (`Finished in 2 minutes 53.5 seconds`)
+- Baseline examples: 1877, 0 failures, seed 42067
+- Final wall time: 96 seconds
+- Final RSpec time: 93.75 seconds (`Finished in 1 minute 33.75 seconds`)
+- Final examples: 1877, 0 failures, seed 38214
+- Wall-clock improvement: 45.5%
+- RSpec-reported improvement: 46.0%
+- Target met: request specs are faster by more than 30% while retaining per-example isolation.
+
+## Request setup changes used
+
+- Request specs default to fake Sidekiq mode. The notification, conversation, home-timeline, and favourite specs that create records through services keep explicit `:sidekiq_inline` metadata, and mail/assertion examples keep inline execution where synchronous delivery is asserted.
+- The cache behavior spec now creates only fixtures needed for the endpoint in the example description. Its eager per-example block was replaced with lazy account, status, private-status, poll, and invite fixtures plus the existing follow relationship needed for private status access.
+- Cache and Redis cleanup remain broad for request examples. Selective cleanup was tested and reverted because cache and Redis state otherwise leaked across unrelated request examples; adding it only to a few files reduced isolation and caused authorization, rate-limit, and stale-response failures.
+- No app runtime code was changed in the setup phase. Factory callback changes are recorded in the follow-up section below.
+
+## Request profile findings
+
+- Baseline slow groups included `ActivityPub Contexts` (4.58 seconds / 7 examples), `ActivityPub Replies` (16.31 seconds / 45 examples), notification APIs, `Caching behavior` (34.37 seconds / 258 examples), signature verification, media, and timeline feeds.
+- The cache behavior spec dropped from 24.28 RSpec seconds / 258 examples (26 wall seconds) to 10.44 RSpec seconds / 258 examples (12 wall seconds) after lazy fixture setup. This is a 57.0% standalone RSpec-speed improvement.
+- Final slow examples remain concentrated in backup generation, ActivityPub contexts/replies, notification API setup, media processing, signature verification, and timeline/feed serialization rather than suite-wide setup hooks.
+- Sidekiq inline execution is now isolated to the request files whose assertions need worker-created notifications, conversation records, timeline entries, favourite state, or delivered mail.
+
+## Combined model and request result
+
+- Baseline RSpec time: 138.7 model seconds + 173.5 request seconds = 312.2 seconds.
+- Final RSpec time: 61.72 model seconds + 93.75 request seconds = 155.47 seconds.
+- Combined RSpec improvement: 50.2%, or 2.008x faster overall.
+- Baseline combined wall time: 140.894 model seconds + 176 request seconds = 316.894 seconds.
+- Final combined wall time: 63.895 model seconds + 96 request seconds = 159.895 seconds.
+- Combined wall-clock improvement: 49.5%.
+
+## Run policy
+
+- Profile and optimize only `spec/models` and `spec/requests`; do not run multiple spec directories together.
+- Do not run system specs or streaming specs.
+- Re-run each target folder separately after setup or factory changes.
+- Re-run the cache behavior file separately when changing cache fixtures or cache cleanup, and re-record future baseline/profile values here.
+
+## Factory optimization follow-up
+
+- Factory baseline command: `bundle exec rspec spec/models --format progress --profile 50`, then `bundle exec rspec spec/requests --format progress --profile 50` (run separately; never combine them).
+- Factory baseline model wall time: 55.184 seconds; RSpec time: 52.98 seconds; 1795 examples, 0 failures, seed 11857.
+- Factory baseline request wall time: 107.438 seconds; RSpec time: 105.22 seconds; 1877 examples, 0 failures, seed 59364.
+- Final model wall time: 41.806 seconds; RSpec time: 39.37 seconds; 1795 examples, 0 failures, seed 8585.
+- Final request wall time: 96.423 seconds; RSpec time: 94.17 seconds; 1877 examples, 0 failures, seed 21007.
+- Factory model improvement: 24.2% wall-clock and 25.7% RSpec-reported.
+- Factory request improvement: 10.3% wall-clock and 10.5% RSpec-reported.
+- Factory combined wall-clock improvement: 15.0% (162.622 seconds to 138.229 seconds).
+- Factory combined RSpec improvement: 15.6% (158.20 seconds to 133.54 seconds).
+
+### Factory change used
+
+- Removed the status fabricator's `after_create` callback that fabricated an unrelated bookmark for every status. Explicit bookmarks used by export and cleanup-policy examples remain explicit, so assertions and test setup are unchanged.
+- Kept the status fabricator's default account association intact. It is required by ActivityPub reply and private-status request setup.
+- Kept the user fabricator's login-activity callback intact. Removing it removed setup that is still relied on by other suite behavior and exposed broader suite-order instability (including account-search failures); a quick full profile showed the status callback alone already exceeded the factory optimization target.
+- No app runtime code or application behavior was changed.
+
+### Factory profile findings
+
+- Status bookkeeping was the largest common factory side effect: every created status also created a bookmark, and that bookmark in turn created additional account/user setup through nested fabricators.
+- Account and status creation remain the dominant setup pattern in model and request specs, but the status callback removal avoided one full extra bookmark/account/user chain per default status.
+- The final model profile still concentrates time in media attachments, trends, cleanup policy, tag/public feeds, notifications, and status/threading setup rather than suite-wide callback overhead.
+- The final request profile still concentrates time in notifications, signature verification, ActivityPub replies/context, feeds, media, and API serialization.
+
+### Factory trials to retain in notes
+
+- Passing `user: nil` in the status fabricator's default account was reverted: isolated AP replies then returned 404 responses in the full request suite.
+- Removing the user fabricator's login-activity callback was reverted after it introduced account-search and suite-order instability. The callback remains useful enough to preserve isolation and runtime behavior.
+- One model rerun during the status-only pass showed only isolated batch-action flakiness in `Form::AccountBatch`; running that file alone passed and the next full model rerun passed all 1795 examples.
